WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.561
In the last lesson, a second clerk stood at the storeroom door.

2
00:00:06.561 --> 00:00:11.300
Before each label was torn off, the clerk copied one word onto a note: apples.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.337
Today we pick up that note and look at it closely.

4
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:19.212
The shop's office keeps a card index, a small drawer of cards.

5
00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:24.239
Each card is filed under a note that names a tool, like Scale or Label printer.

6
00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:26.657
The card says where to find that tool.

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.056
Then the shop opens a second shelf.

8
00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:31.957
One shelf holds apples, and one holds lemons.

9
00:00:31.957 --> 00:00:34.794
But on a one-word note, both are just Shelf.

10
00:00:34.794 --> 00:00:37.759
We file the apple shelf, then the lemon shelf.

11
00:00:37.759 --> 00:00:40.145
How many cards are in the drawer now?

12
00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:41.176
Pause and guess.

13
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:44.465
One.

14
00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:48.501
The second card pushed the first one out, and nobody noticed.

15
00:00:48.501 --> 00:00:52.206
Nobody, until someone fetched lemons for an apple order.

16
00:00:52.206 --> 00:00:56.109
Somewhere, a customer is biting into a very sour apple pie.

17
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:57.661
So the point is this.

18
00:00:57.661 --> 00:01:01.577
The note had room for one word, and one word wasn't enough.

19
00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:04.231
In code, that one-word note is a KClass.

20
00:01:04.231 --> 00:01:10.736
And the trap is easy to fall into, because for plain classes, a class behaves exactly like a type.

21
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:14.602
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

22
00:01:14.602 --> 00:01:15.751
Kotlin has KClass.

23
00:01:15.751 --> 00:01:16.708
Java has Class.

24
00:01:16.708 --> 00:01:19.260
And then there's something called KType.

25
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.068
Aren't those three names for the same thing?

26
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.581
Let's start from what we already know.

27
00:01:25.581 --> 00:01:29.152
Earlier in this track, we pulled classes and types apart.

28
00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:34.038
The class String backs two types: String, and String?, which may also be null.

29
00:01:34.038 --> 00:01:37.797
The class List backs List&lt;String&gt;, List&lt;Int&gt;, and many more.

30
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.079
Then came erasure.

31
00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:45.124
The compiler checks each type argument, the real type in the angle brackets, and then drops it.

32
00:01:45.124 --> 00:01:49.515
So while the program runs, a box on the shelf still knows it's a box.

33
00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:51.997
It no longer knows what its label said.

34
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:56.660
So a running program gets three different objects.

35
00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.330
The first is a KClass.

36
00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.579
It describes a class, like String::class or List::class.

37
00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.373
It has no type arguments, and it's never nullable.

38
00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:08.345
This is our one-word note.

39
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:12.909
The second is a Class, Java's object for the same class.

40
00:02:12.909 --> 00:02:18.065
In Java you write String.class, and in Kotlin, String::class.java.

41
00:02:18.065 --> 00:02:21.503
It's the same note, written on Java's paper.

42
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:23.026
The third is a KType.

43
00:02:23.026 --> 00:02:28.463
It describes a full type: the class, its type arguments, and whether it may be null.

44
00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:30.016
You get one from typeOf.

45
00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.576
This is the whole label: a list of strings, or nothing.

46
00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:34.651
Let's map it.

47
00:02:34.651 --> 00:02:36.770
The one-word note is a KClass.

48
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.665
The same note on Java's paper is a Class.

49
00:02:39.665 --> 00:02:41.571
The whole label is a KType.

50
00:02:41.571 --> 00:02:47.079
And the box on the shelf is the object itself, which only remembers its class.

51
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.510
Here's the lesson's demo, in Kotlin.

52
00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.717
k is a KClass, c is a Class, and t is a KType.

53
00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:55.599
Then it asks two questions.

54
00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:00.061
Do an ArrayList of String and an ArrayList of Int share a class?

55
00:03:00.061 --> 00:03:02.711
And is String the same type as String?

56
00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:03.951
Same class?

57
00:03:03.951 --> 00:03:04.936
Same type?

58
00:03:04.936 --> 00:03:06.513
Pause and guess.

59
00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.123
Here's what it printed.

60
00:03:12.123 --> 00:03:13.563
Share a class: true.

61
00:03:13.563 --> 00:03:15.939
One class, ArrayList, backs both.

62
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:17.956
Same type: false, and false.

63
00:03:17.956 --> 00:03:22.708
The nullable String is a different type, and so is a list of ints.

64
00:03:22.708 --> 00:03:30.197
And the KType knows its parts: its classifier is List::class, it has one argument, and nullable is true.

65
00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:31.693
Notice one odd thing.

66
00:03:31.693 --> 00:03:36.354
Two lines end with a little apology: Kotlin reflection is not available.

67
00:03:36.354 --> 00:03:41.404
It may be the most polite message you'll ever see, and it isn't even an error.

68
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.859
Nothing is broken.

69
00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:49.644
To print Kotlin names, like kotlin.String, toString needs an extra library called kotlin-reflect.

70
00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:53.352
A Gradle project leaves it out unless you ask for it.

71
00:03:53.352 --> 00:03:56.709
So Kotlin falls back to Java names, and says so.

72
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.034
Now let's be skeptical.

73
00:03:59.034 --> 00:04:03.710
If the clerk could copy one word onto a note, why not copy the whole label?

74
00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.516
Why can't I just ask a box for its full type?

75
00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:07.918
Watch the arrows.

76
00:04:07.918 --> 00:04:14.616
From an object at run time, x::class reaches the KClass, and x.javaClass reaches the Class.

77
00:04:14.616 --> 00:04:16.530
Nothing reaches the KType.

78
00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:23.818
A KType comes from code: the compiler writes it down at a call site, where it still knows the type.

79
00:04:23.818 --> 00:04:25.879
typeOf is how you ask it to.

80
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:28.746
And here the clerk picture breaks a little.

81
00:04:28.746 --> 00:04:32.172
A real clerk could copy the full label onto every note.

82
00:04:32.172 --> 00:04:37.841
Kotlin writes a KType only where you ask for one, and it never stores it inside the object.

83
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:42.102
So, to pick the right one, ask what you want to know.

84
00:04:42.102 --> 00:04:43.818
What kind of thing is this?

85
00:04:43.818 --> 00:04:48.139
That's a question about a class, and a KClass or a Class answers it.

86
00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:49.093
A list of what?

87
00:04:49.093 --> 00:04:50.046
May it be null?

88
00:04:50.046 --> 00:04:54.304
Those are questions about a type, and only a KType can answer them.

89
00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.150
Now the name for all this.

90
00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:02.558
The part of the library that lets code inspect classes while the program runs is called reflection.

91
00:05:02.558 --> 00:05:05.729
KClass, Class and KType are three of its objects.

92
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.583
One guardrail.

93
00:05:07.583 --> 00:05:10.673
You can't ask for the class of a whole type.

94
00:05:10.673 --> 00:05:18.819
Write Shelf&lt;Apple&gt;::class, and the compiler says: only classes are allowed on the left-hand side of a class literal.

95
00:05:18.819 --> 00:05:23.032
A class literal is the double colon class you write in code.

96
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.005
So here's the rule of thumb.

97
00:05:25.005 --> 00:05:31.192
Key a container by KClass when the things in it are plain, like the scale and the label printer.

98
00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:35.188
Key it by KType when they are generic, like a shelf of apples.

99
00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:37.267
Back to the drawer.

100
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:44.151
File the shelves under typeOf&lt;Shelf&lt;Apple&gt;&gt;() and typeOf&lt;Shelf&lt;Lemon&gt;&gt;(), and the two cards sit side by side.

101
00:05:44.151 --> 00:05:46.867
The lemons finally get a card of their own.

102
00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:50.088
The last section of the lesson builds exactly that.

103
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.313
So, are KClass, Class and KType the same thing?

104
00:05:54.313 --> 00:05:58.401
Two of them are: one note, on Kotlin's paper or on Java's.

105
00:05:58.401 --> 00:06:00.445
The third is the whole label.

106
00:06:00.445 --> 00:06:02.208
Objects know their class.

107
00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:04.111
Only code knows full types.

108
00:06:04.267 --> 00:06:05.060
Quick check.

109
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.506
Take String, and the nullable String.

110
00:06:07.506 --> 00:06:09.357
Do they have the same class?

111
00:06:09.357 --> 00:06:11.141
Do they have the same type?

112
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.123
Same class.

113
00:06:15.123 --> 00:06:19.787
The demo printed true: a nullable String is backed by the class String.

114
00:06:19.787 --> 00:06:23.465
But different types, because a KType counts nullability.
